Chapter: FieldGuardRefine

 A comparison narrowed a bare LOCAL's range inside the arm it guards,
 and nothing else. So `if b.val <= 15 then Nibble { n = b.val }` read
 b.val as its declared 0..255 and was rejected, while the identical
 `let v = b.val in if v <= 15 then Nibble { n = v }` was proven and its
 check elided. Same guard, same value, same store: the only difference
 was that one was a name and the other was not.

Section: Definitions

  Byte = record {
   val : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  Nibble = record {
   n : Integer between 0 and 15
  }

  guarded-field : Byte -> Integer
  guarded-field (b) =
   if b.val <= 15 then (Nibble { n = b.val }).n else 0

  guarded-local : Byte -> Integer
  guarded-local (b) =
   let v = b.val
   in if v <= 15 then (Nibble { n = v }).n else 0

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (guarded-field (Byte { val = 9 })))
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (guarded-local (Byte { val = 9 })))
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (guarded-field (Byte { val = 200 })))
  end
